return array (
  'content' => '<p>Custom metal fabrication at Vyomika Atelier follows a predictable sequence from first brief to site handover — whether the item is a PVD partition in Gurgaon, a main entrance door in Hyderabad or Corten screens in Goa. Understanding the process helps architects programme interiors realistically and homeowners know when to lock dimensions.</p><h2>Enquiry and brief review</h2><p>Share plans, photos, inspiration references and finish preferences via <a href="/contact">contact</a> or <a href="/professionals">professionals</a> for trade enquiries. We confirm scope fit — partitions, doors, railings, Corten or furniture.</p><h2>Concept and budget alignment</h2><p>Initial guidance on system type, glass and finish narrows cost drivers without promising fixed catalogue rates. Complex items may need paid site visit before firm quote in distant cities.</p><h2>Site measurement or dimension confirmation</h2><p>Final dimensions taken after floor screed and ceiling finish where possible. Renovation projects measure existing openings with laser level notes.</p><p>Partition and door guides: <a href="/studio/pvd-partitions">PVD partitions</a>, <a href="/studio/main-entrance-pvd-doors">entrance doors</a>.</p><h2>Shop drawings and sample approval</h2><p>Detailed drawings show panel splits, hardware, fixing plates and glass make-up. PVD finish sample approved before batch production.</p><p>Architect sign-off recommended on commercial projects; residential clients approve via marked PDF.</p><h2>Fabrication at Delhi studio</h2><p>CNC cutting, welding, grinding, PVD chamber batch and glass assembly under QC check. Corten items may weather partially before dispatch depending on scope.</p><h2>Delivery, installation and handover</h2><p>Packed transport pan-India; client contractor or agreed installer fixes per drawing. Handover includes care notes for PVD and glass — not generic guarantees.</p><p>See completed work on <a href="/projects">projects</a>.</p><h2>Revision control on shop drawings</h2><p>Mark approved PDFs with date and revision number. Site changes after approval should flow through formal revision — verbal tweaks cause expensive remakes on CNC-cut parts.</p><p>Architect and client sign-off paths differ on commercial versus residential projects — agree who can release fabrication in writing.</p><h2>Quality control before dispatch</h2><p>QC photography of welded joints, PVD faces and glass make-up before crating helps resolve transit disputes. Label each crate with room, orientation and fragile face arrows.</p><p>Corten items may ship with partial patina depending on scope — note expected on-site weathering in handover pack.</p><h2>Installation sequencing on site</h2><p>Install partitions after floor and ceiling substrates are true; install entrance doors after stone thresholds are set. Protect finished PVD until snagging complete.</p><p>Client contractor install is acceptable with approved drawings — fabricator remains available for technical clarification without implying nationwide install coverage.</p><h2>Post-handover support</h2><p>Handover includes care notes for PVD and glass — not open-ended performance guarantees beyond documented scope.</p><p>International enquiries are reviewed project by project — crating, freight and import coordination quoted separately.</p><h2>Quotation inputs that speed accurate pricing</h2><p>Floor plans with dimensions, elevation sketches, preferred glass type, finish direction and photos of adjacent materials allow faster quotation than mood boards alone. Note city for logistics planning and whether install is supply-only or includes site fixing support.</p><h2>Production release gate</h2><p>Fabrication should not start until shop drawings are approved and finish sample is signed. Rush orders without sample approval create the highest rework rate on PVD batches and custom glass sizes.</p><h3>Partial deliveries and phased sites</h3><p>Large projects may phase delivery by floor or wing. Label crates clearly and align payment milestones with dispatch phases if contract requires — reduces site storage damage on finished metal.</p><h2>Snagging and touch-up policy</h2><p>Minor transit scuffs may be touchable on hairline finishes; mirror faces may need component replacement. Agree snagging response process in quotation letter rather than assuming open-ended site revisits nationwide.</p><h2>Link to architect specification</h2><p>Architects briefing metalwork should read <a href="/blog/architects-specify-custom-architectural-metalwork">how to specify custom architectural metalwork</a> in parallel with this process overview so drawings contain the information fabrication needs.</p><h2>Roles and responsibilities on site</h2><p>Define who receives crates, who stores finished metal before install, and who has authority to accept or reject delivery condition. Uncrated panels leaning against unfinished walls are a common source of finish damage unrelated to fabrication quality.</p><p>Supply-only projects still benefit from a pre-install call to review fixing sequence — especially for ceiling-mounted partition tracks and heavy entrance door leaves. Architects specifying scope should align tender language with <a href="/blog/architects-specify-custom-architectural-metalwork">specification guidance</a> so contractors know what documentation accompanies delivery.</p><h2>Payment milestones tied to documentation</h2><p>Many projects align deposit with drawing approval, progress payment with fabrication start, and balance with dispatch or successful site fix. Clear milestones reduce disputes when scope changes mid-programme — each revision should note whether timeline shifts.</p><p>Retain approved PDFs and sample photos in project cloud storage so later maintenance teams know exact specifications years after handover.</p><h2>Remote coordination for out-of-city projects</h2><p>Projects outside Delhi NCR often proceed with dimensioned surveys, photos and video walkthroughs before fabrication release. Confirm who verifies critical dimensions on site when the fabricator team is not present locally — mismeasurement at this stage drives most avoidable remakes.</p><p>Confirm insurance coverage for high-value glass-and-metal crates during transit — project-specific policies vary and should be aligned before dispatch of large partition packages.</p><h2>Client visibility during fabrication</h2><p>Progress photos at key stages — welded frame, pre-PVD, crated dispatch — help remote clients on out-of-city projects stay aligned without site visits. Agree photo milestones in the quotation letter so expectations on communication are clear from the start.</p><p>Schedule installation crews only when fixing surfaces are ready — premature fixes into unset plaster or green screed cause alignment issues that are blamed on metal fabrication unfairly.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>From drawing to installation, custom metalwork needs measured dimensions, approved shop drawings and finish samples before fabrication begins. Programme site readiness before ordering — rework costs more than waiting one week for final floor levels.</p><p>Begin at <a href="/contact">contact</a>.</p><p>For architects, see <a href="/blog/architects-specify-custom-architectural-metalwork">specification guide</a>.</p><p>Explore <a href="/studio/pvd-partitions">PVD partitions</a>.</p><p>For a project-specific quotation, share floor plans or photos through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Architects and interior designers may also begin from the <a href="/professionals">professionals collaboration page</a>.</p>',
